{
	title: 'Tab strips: equal cells, underline, pages; full-width web',
	spec: `Tab strips in the terminal and on the web are one row of equal cells in real tab order, so Ctrl-N/P always move one cell right/left and no number ever shifts.

Cells: every cell has the same width: the widest tab number, right-aligned, plus one marker slot (task fr's glyph, or a blank of the same width) and a gap. The current tab is shown by an underline on its number (colour of the active tab), never by brackets or padding that would move other cells. The terminal loses its [n] brackets.

Names: only when every tab fits with a name at one fixed width (number, marker, name clipped to that width) do cells show names; otherwise all cells are numbers. No per-tab close button in the strip (the web closes with Ctrl-W, /close or the full list).

Pages: when not even every number fits, tabs come in fixed pages of as many cells as fit beside two edge buttons: tabs 1..k, k+1..2k, and so on. The shown page is the one holding the current tab, a pure function of (tab count, current index, cells that fit), so going somewhere and back looks identical; moving inside a page moves only the underline, crossing a border replaces the row. The edge buttons have a fixed width (widest count plus arrow plus marker slot): the left one reads ‹N, the right one N›, N being how many tabs lie on that side, followed by the most urgent marker among them (failed over asking or attention over working; finished marks do not count), hidden when that side is empty. Clicking an edge goes to the neighbouring page (the web; in the terminal they are display only). Ctrl-N on the last tab wraps to the first.

The paging arithmetic (which page, which edge counts, which most-urgent mark) lives in one common module used by both clients, tested as a pure function.

Web: the strip is one row, a menu button (the existing sheet: the full list with close buttons, now on every width) then the cells, edge buttons and new tab button; it never wraps and never scrolls sideways (the back-swipe rule of task ng). The whole header stays under 50px at any width. The current tab's name is in the status row already, so there is no title line.

Terminal: the tab bar keeps its hints and 'Tabs:' label while they fit (task 7w), then drops them, then pages, and never clips a cell.

Web transcript and composer use the full window width (minus padding), text included: no reading-width cap (the user: text often loves a wide screen).`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'Mocks from the design discussion: /tmp/tabs-pages.html (the chosen paged strip) and /tmp/tabs-lens.html (rejected: variable-width cells and a sliding window made numbers move). Rejected also: centring on the current tab (every step shifts all numbers) and a lazily sliding window (the view depends on history).'
	],
	needs: ['ng', '7w', 'fr']
}
